An audio processing device may synchronize an apparent depth of one or more sound objects with an accurately renderable depth range of a stereoscopic or three-dimensional image display as follows:

**1. An audio processing device comprising:**

- an input interface configured to receive an audio signal including at least one sound object and associated sound-object position information;
- a display interface configured to obtain display-depth information indicative of an accurately renderable depth range of a stereoscopic or three-dimensional image display, the accurately renderable depth range being bounded by a near depth limit and a far depth limit relative to a display plane;
- one or more processors configured to:
  - determine an intended depth coordinate of the at least one sound object;
  - establish a depth-mapping function based at least in part on the near depth limit and the far depth limit;
  - transform the intended depth coordinate into a mapped depth coordinate lying within the accurately renderable depth range;
  - derive, from the mapped depth coordinate, one or more auditory depth-rendering parameters; and
  - process the audio signal in accordance with the auditory depth-rendering parameters such that a perceived depth of the at least one sound object substantially corresponds to a visually presented depth of an associated image object; and
- an output interface configured to provide the processed audio signal to one or more acoustic transducers.

**2.** The audio processing device of claim 1, wherein the depth-mapping function comprises a linear, piecewise-linear, nonlinear, logarithmic, or perceptually weighted mapping between an source depth range and the accurately renderable depth range.

**3.** The audio processing device of claim 1, wherein the one or more processors are further configured to constrain the mapped depth coordinate to the near depth limit or the far depth limit when the intended depth coordinate lies outside the accurately renderable depth range.

**4.** The audio processing device of claim 1, wherein the auditory depth-rendering parameters comprise at least one of direct-to-reverberant energy ratio, propagation delay, sound-pressure level, spectral attenuation, high-frequency roll-off, early-reflection pattern, reverberation time, binaural disparity, head-related transfer function parameters, wavefront curvature, or loudspeaker-array focusing parameters.

**5.** The audio processing device of claim 1, wherein the display-depth information is determined from at least one of display geometry, stereoscopic disparity limits, viewer position, viewing distance, screen size, accommodation-vergence constraints, image-rendering metadata, or a display calibration profile.

**6.** The audio processing device of claim 1, wherein the one or more processors are configured to dynamically update the depth-mapping function in response to a change in viewer position, display configuration, image depth, or sound-object position.

**7.** The audio processing device of claim 1, wherein, when the at least one sound object is associated with an image object, the mapped depth coordinate is determined from a rendered depth coordinate of the image object, thereby maintaining audiovisual depth correspondence.

**8. A method of synchronizing auditory and visual depth, the method comprising:**

receiving audio-object metadata specifying an intended position of a sound object;

obtaining display-depth information defining a range of depths that are accurately renderable by a stereoscopic or three-dimensional image display;

mapping an intended depth of the sound object to a target depth within said range;

determining audio-rendering parameters representative of the target depth;

rendering an audio signal associated with the sound object using the determined audio-rendering parameters; and

outputting the rendered audio signal such that the sound object is perceived at a depth substantially coincident with, or perceptually consistent with, a displayed visual object.

The mapping may preserve relative depth ordering among a plurality of sound objects while compressing, expanding, offsetting, or clipping their respective intended depths to conform to the display’s accurately renderable depth range. Temporal smoothing or hysteresis may further be applied to changes in mapped depth to reduce audible discontinuities during dynamic depth remapping.
